  <title>Switches</title>
  <p>A switch is a VDE switch, as an Ethernet switch: the other bricks plug into its ports. A virtual machine plugs a network card into it, a wire joins it to another switch, a tap joins this computer to it. A switch needs nothing else to start. It runs <cmd>vde_switch</cmd>.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The settings of sw1, with tap1 and vm1 plugged into it</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/switch-ports.png">
      <p>The settings of sw1: Ports, 32, with the line Number of ports · at least 3: tap1, vm1 plug into sw1 under it; Hub mode and Fast spanning tree, off.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <section id="ports">
    <title>Ports</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Ports</gui></title>
        <p>How many plugs the switch takes, from 1 to 128; 32 by default. Each plug takes a port: a machine with two cards in the switch takes two. The switch can't have fewer ports than the plugs in it, and the line under <gui>Ports</gui> says how many it needs and which bricks plug into it.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Hub mode</gui></title>
        <p>Send every packet to every port, as a hub does, not only to the port of its destination. A program that captures packets on one port, as <cmd>tcpdump</cmd> in a machine, then sees the traffic of all of them. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Fast spanning tree</gui></title>
        <p>Run the fast spanning tree protocol, which finds the loops between switches and turns off the ports that close them, so that a packet doesn't go round forever. Turn it on in the switches of a lab whose wires make a loop. Off by default.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="running">
    <title>While it runs</title>
    <p>The three settings change at once in a switch that runs: Virtualbricks gives them to <cmd>vde_switch</cmd> through its console, without stopping it, and the bar over the settings says so. The bricks plugged into the switch stay plugged.</p>
    <p>In the console of the switch, <guiseq><gui>Process</gui><gui>Open Control Monitor</gui></guiseq> in its menu, <cmd>port/print</cmd> lists its ports and what is plugged into each; see <link xref="bricks#process"/>.</p>
  </section>
